# CyberArk Vaulted Account Field Updater

## Description

`Update-VaultedAccounts.ps1` updates CyberArk Privilege Cloud vaulted accounts using one CSV file:

```text
samples/account-update-template.csv
```

The script reads each row, compares the current vaulted account to the CSV target values, then chooses the flow automatically.

Supported flows:

- patch existing account fields
- create replacement account when Safe changes
- recreate account when platform device type changes
- keep the old/source account by default
- write recreated accounts and delete candidates to reports
- optionally disable CPM on the old/source account
- optionally delete the old/source account only with explicit confirmation

## Dependencies

- Windows PowerShell 5.1 or later
- Network access to CyberArk Identity and Privilege Cloud / PVWA
- CyberArk Identity OAuth service user
- API permissions to read/update/create accounts, read Safes, read platforms, and retrieve secrets when recreate is required
- Proxy access if required by the server

## File Layout

```text
CyberArk-AccountFieldUpdater/
  Update-VaultedAccounts.ps1
  .env
  env/
    dev.env
    prod.env
  samples/
    account-update-template.csv
  logs/
  reports/
  README.md
```

## Environment Files

`.env`

```ini
TENANT_ID="abq4291"
SUB_DOMAIN="mtbanktest"
PROXY_ADDRESS="proxy.company.com"
PROXY_PORT="8080"
```

`env/dev.env` or `env/prod.env`

```ini
PROXY="http://proxy.company.com:8080"
PVWA="https://mtbanktest.privilegecloud.cyberark.cloud"
```

Use the tenant root URL for `PVWA`. The script normalizes it to `/PasswordVault/API`.

## CSV File

Use one CSV only:

```text
samples/account-update-template.csv
```

Leave unused columns blank. Add additional platform properties as `Prop:<PropertyName>`.

## CSV Columns

| Column | Required | Purpose |
|---|---:|---|
| `AccountID` | Yes | Existing CyberArk account ID |
| `TargetSafeName` | No | Target Safe. If blank, current Safe is used |
| `TargetPlatformID` | No | Target platform. If blank, current platform is used |
| `TargetName` | No | New CyberArk account object name |
| `TargetAddress` | No | New address |
| `TargetUserName` | No | New username |
| `AutomaticManagementEnabled` | No | `true` or `false` |
| `DisableAutomaticManagement` | No | `true` or `false` |
| `ManualManagementReason` | No | CPM manual management reason |
| `Prop:<PropertyName>` | No | Target platform account property |

Included template columns:

```text
Prop:LogonDomain
Prop:Port
Prop:Domain
Prop:Database
Prop:Instance
Prop:URL
```

Unsupported `Prop:` columns are ignored and written to the log.

## Usage

### Dev

```powershell
.\Update-VaultedAccounts.ps1 -Environment dev
```

### Prod

```powershell
.\Update-VaultedAccounts.ps1 -Environment prod
```

### Prod with proxy credentials

```powershell
.\Update-VaultedAccounts.ps1 -Environment prod -ProxyUseDefaultCredentials
```

### Prod and disable CPM on old/source accounts after recreate

```powershell
.\Update-VaultedAccounts.ps1 -Environment prod -DisableSourceAfterMigration
```

### Prod and delete old/source accounts after approval

```powershell
.\Update-VaultedAccounts.ps1 `
  -Environment prod `
  -DisableSourceAfterMigration `
  -DeleteSourceAfterMigration `
  -ConfirmDeleteSourceAfterMigration YES
```

## How the Script Chooses the Flow

| Detected CSV change | Script action |
|---|---|
| Same Safe, same device type | PATCH existing account |
| Safe changed | Create replacement account in target Safe |
| Platform changed, same device type | PATCH/update when possible |
| Platform changed, different device type | Recreate account with target platform |

For Safe changes and device-type migrations, the script retrieves the existing secret only for the recreate operation. The secret is not printed or written to logs/reports.

## After Recreate

After Safe change or device-type migration, there will normally be two accounts:

| Account | Status |
|---|---|
| New account | Created in target Safe/platform |
| Old/source account | Kept for review by default |

The old/source account is not deleted unless both are supplied:

```powershell
-DeleteSourceAfterMigration -ConfirmDeleteSourceAfterMigration YES
```

To keep the old/source account but disable CPM management:

```powershell
-DisableSourceAfterMigration
```

## Reports

Each run creates:

```text
logs/Update-VaultedAccounts_*.log
reports/Update-VaultedAccounts_Report_*.csv
reports/Update-VaultedAccounts_Recreated_*.csv
reports/Update-VaultedAccounts_DeleteCandidates_*.csv
```

| Report | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `Update-VaultedAccounts_Report_*.csv` | Main result per CSV row |
| `Update-VaultedAccounts_Recreated_*.csv` | Newly recreated accounts |
| `Update-VaultedAccounts_DeleteCandidates_*.csv` | Old/source accounts to review |

## Notes

- Use one CSV: `samples/account-update-template.csv`.
- Leave unused columns blank.
- `TargetAddress` satisfies required `Address`.
- `TargetUserName` satisfies required `Username`.
- Unsupported `Prop:` values are ignored and logged.
- Source accounts are kept unless delete confirmation is supplied.
- Do not commit real `.env`, `dev.env`, `prod.env`, logs, reports, or production CSV data to Git.
